\id GAL - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.5
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Galatians
\toc1 Paul’s letter to the assemblies in Galatia
\toc2 Galatians
\mt2 Paul’s letter to the assemblies in
\mt1 Galatia
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\p
\v 1 \add This letter is from\add* Paul, one \add of Jesus’\add* ambassadors, not chosen by people or any human desire, but chosen \add directly\add* by Jesus \add the\add* messiah and God the father who raised Jesus from the dead.
\v 2 \add On behalf of\add* all the believers here, \add I’m writing\add* to the assemblies \add of believers\add* in Galatia.
\p
\v 3 May you all experience the grace and peace that comes from God the father and our master Jesus \add the\add* messiah,
\v 4 the one who gave himself for our sins to rescue us from this present, evil age, according to God our father’s desire—
\v 5 \add our father\add* who’ll be praised even through future ages. That’s how it is.
\s1 There’s only one good message
\p
\v 6 I’m astounded that you \add people\add* have so quickly replaced the good message \add that you heard\add* from the one who called you to the messiah’s grace. You’ve replaced it with another narrative
\v 7 which is not at all another good message, but rather comes from those who are confusing you and wanting to alter the \add actual\add* good message about the messiah.
\v 8 Even if either we ourselves or \add even\add* a messenger from heaven were to preach a different ‘good message’ which disagrees with the good message that we \add first\add* preached to you, then they should be cursed.
\v 9 As we’ve previously said, and even now say it again: if anyone preaches a ‘good message’ to you that’s different from what you already heard, let that person be cursed.
\p
\v 10 Am I trying to please and persuade people or God? If I was still trying to please people, I wouldn’t be \add worthy to be\add* one of \add the\add* messiah’s slaves.
\s1 Paul was commissioned directly by God
\p
\v 11 I’m letting you know, brothers and sisters, that the good message I declared didn’t just come from human ideas.
\v 12 It didn’t come from my ideas, nor was I taught it by some other person, but it was revealed to me by Jesus \add the\add* messiah.
\p
\v 13 \add Brothers and sisters,\add* you’ve heard about my previous behaviour, when as a strict Jew, I was extreme in persecuting the assembly of God’s people, and was trying to destroy them.
\v 14 I was progressing in Judaism quicker than many of my contemporaries, being so much more zealous in observing our ancient traditions.
\p
\v 15 But God, who’d planned this from even before I was born, called me, because of his grace,
\v 16 so that he could reveal his son through me, and I could preach the good message to non-Jews. I didn’t rush to get advice from others,
\v 17 nor did I head to Jerusalem to \add consult with\add* the ambassadors there that preceded me, but I went away to Arabia and then later returned to Damascus.
\v 18 Then \add finally\add*, after three years, I went to Jerusalem to get to know Cephas, and stayed with him for just over two weeks.
\v 19 I didn’t meet any of the other ambassadors, other than Jesus’s brother, James,
\v 20 and what I’m writing to you, I’m writing with God watching, so I’m not lying.
\p
\v 21 Then I went into the regions of Syria and Cilicia.
\v 22 The assemblies back in Judea that believe in \add the\add* messiah didn’t know me by sight,
\v 23 but had only heard about me—that the person who’d once been persecuting them was now preaching the good message about the faith himself,
\v 24 and they praised God because of \add what had happened to\add* me.
\c 2
\s1 The ambassadors accept Paul and Barnabas
\p
\v 1 It was fourteen years later that I made the uphill trek to Jerusalem with Barnabas, taking Titus along as well.
\v 2 \add God\add* had told me to go there, and I explained to the \add leaders there\add* the good message that I’d been declaring to the non-Jews. I did it privately, to double-check that I wasn’t somehow just acting vainly.
\v 3 But not even Titus, who was with me, was compelled to be circumcised, even though he’s a Greek.
\v 4 However, some false brothers were sneaked in to monitor the freedoms that we have in \add the\add* messiah, Jesus, and they wanted us to be enslaved to their traditions.
\v 5 But we didn’t submit to them even for one hour, so that the truth of the good message could continue amongst you.
\p
\v 6 And those people who were supposed \add leaders\add* (although what they were didn’t mean much to me, because God doesn’t go by appearances) didn’t \add really\add* contribute anything useful, as far as I could tell.
\v 7 Instead, they saw that I’ve been entrusted with taking the good message to the non-Jews, just like Peter was entrusted to take it to the Jews,
\v 8 because the same God who worked in Peter to assign him to the Jews also worked in me \add to assign me\add* to the non-Jews.
\v 9 So, seeing the grace that \add God\add* had shown towards me, James and Cephas and John, the apparent pillars \add of the assemblies\add*, accepted me and Barnabas as equals, so that we could go to the non-Jews and them to the Jews.
\v 10 Also, \add they said that\add* we need to keep helping the poor, but I was eager to do that anyway.
\s1 Paul scolds Cephas
\p
\v 11 But when Cephas came to Antioch, I had to oppose him to his face, because he’d messed up.
\v 12 He’d been eating with non-Jews, but when James and his companions arrived, he about-faced and started eating separately, because he was afraid of \add being criticised by\add* the strict Jews.
\v 13 The other Jews with him were also being hypocrites—so much so that even Barnabas joined their hypocrisy—
\v 14 but when I saw that they weren’t being honest with the truth of the good message, I had to say to Cephas in front of everybody, “If you Jews are living more like non-Jews, and not like \add strict\add* Jews, how can you force non-Jews to obey all the Jewish traditions?”
\s1 You can’t be saved by obeying rules
\p
\v 15 We follow Jewish laws, not other nations’ customs,
\v 16 but we know that we’re not saved by following rules, but only by faith in Jesus \add the\add* messiah. So we’ve believed in Jesus \add the\add* messiah so that we can be made guiltless by our faith in \add the\add* messiah, and not by obeying all those rules, because none of those rules can ever make any person guiltless.
\v 17 However, if we attempt to be made guiltless through \add the\add* messiah, yet find that we’re sinners, does that mean that \add the\add* messiah is a servant to sin? That could never be so!
\v 18 If I rebuild what I’ve already torn down, that would show me to be a law-breaker.
\v 19 It was the law that showed me that I needed die to the idea of following the law, so that I could live \add to serve\add* God \add instead\add*. I was executed on a post along with \add the\add* messiah,
\v 20 and I’m not living any more, but it’s \add the\add* messiah now living in me. The way that my body stays alive now is by living by faith in God’s son—the one who loved me and allowed himself to be executed for me.
\v 21 I’m not rejecting God’s grace, because if the law could make me guiltless, that would mean that \add the\add* messiah died unnecessarily.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 You foolish Galatians, who tricked you into changing? You know that Jesus \add the\add* messiah was publicly known to have been executed \add by being nailed to\add* a post.
\v 2 So I just want to ask you one thing: did you receive the spirit by obeying all the rules, or was it by faith when you heard?
\v 3 So aren’t you being foolish? Having begun in \add the\add* spirit, now you \add people\add* are focusing back on bodily discipline.
\v 4 You vainly endured so many things—it was vain wasn’t it?
\v 5 So the one who gave the spirit to you and worked miracles among you, did all that come about from obeying the rules or by the faith that came from hearing \add the good message\add*?
\s1 The spreading of Abraham’s blessing
\p
\v 6 Abraham believed in God and was declared guiltless as a result.
\v 7 So now anyone who exercises faith is a descendant of Abraham.
\v 8 The \add writers of the\add* scriptures foresaw that God was going to make non-Jews guiltless through faith when he promised to Abraham that all the nations would be blessed through him,
\v 9 so those who have faith \add that Jesus\add* \add is the messiah\add* will be blessed just as Abraham was blessed for believing \add God\add*.
\p
\v 10 All those who strive to be saved by obeying the law are under a curse, because it’s written that ‘everyone is cursed who doesn’t live by and fulfil everything that’s been written down in the law scroll’.
\v 11 It’s clear that the law can’t make anyone guiltless in God’s view, because \add it’s written that\add* ‘those who are guiltless will live by faith’.
\v 12 And the law doesn’t produce faith, but ‘the person who obeys these things is the one who will live’.
\p
\v 13 \add The\add* messiah saved us from the law’s curse by becoming the curse for our sake, because it’s written, ‘anyone who’s hung on a tree is cursed’.
\v 14 He \add paid the price for us\add* so that the blessing for Abraham would \add also\add* reach the non-Jews through \add the\add* messiah Jesus and we’d \add all\add* be able to receive the spirit through faith.
\s1 The law didn’t annul the promise
\p
\v 15 Brothers and sisters, consider our human ways: once an agreement has been accepted, one party can’t just reject it or add conditions.
\v 16 The promises were given to Abraham and to his descendant. It doesn’t say ‘descendants (plural)’ but is only referring to the one who’s the messiah.
\v 17 So what I’m telling you is that the agreement \add with Abraham\add* that was previously ratified by God was not annulled 430 years later when the law was given \add to Moses\add*, and nor was the promise nullified,
\v 18 because if the inheritance \add now\add* came by obeying the law, then it could no longer be the result of the promise, but God had already granted it to Abraham via the promise.
\v 19 So what was the law for then? Because of sinfulness, it was added until the promised descendant would come. It was directed through messengers and entrusted to \add Moses as\add* a mediator.
\v 20 You don’t need a mediator if there’s only one party involved, and God is one.
\s1 Coming of age for the inheritance
\p
\v 21 So does the law oppose God’s promises? Certainly not, because if the law was able to give life, you could become guiltless by obeying the law.
\v 22 The scriptures \add explain that\add* everyone is imprisoned by sin so that by faith in Jesus \add the\add* messiah, the promise could be granted to those who believe.
\p
\v 23 Before faith could come, we were guarded under the law—imprisoned so that faith could be revealed.
\v 24 So the law was our tutor until \add the\add* messiah came so that we could then be made guiltless by faith,
\v 25 and now that we’ve come to faith, we’re no longer under our tutor.
\p
\v 26 So you’re all God’s children through faith in \add the\add* messiah, Jesus,
\v 27 because everyone who’s been immersed \add to obey\add* \add the\add* messiah is now cloaked in \add the\add* messiah.
\v 28 So there’s no Jew or Greek, no slave or free person, no male or female, because you’re all \add identified\add* as being in \add the\add* messiah, Jesus.
\v 29 And if you’re in \add the\add* messiah, then you’re \add now\add* Abraham’s descendant, and so you’re heirs of that promise.
\c 4
\p
\v 1 But as long as the heir is underage, he’s no wealthier than a slave (even though he’ll \add become\add* the master),
\v 2 but he’s under guardians and managers until he reaches the age that his father specified.
\v 3 That’s how it is with us—when we were infants we were enslaved by this world’s principles,
\v 4 but at the appointed time, God sent his son down to be given birth to by a woman and to live under Moses’ law
\v 5 so that he could buy back those enslaved under the law and they could now be adopted as children \add with an inheritance\add*.
\p
\v 6 So because you’re \add now\add* children, God sent his son’s spirit down into our hearts as we call out, ‘Father, father.’
\v 7 Now you’re no longer slaves, but children \add with full rights\add*, and as children, heirs of God’s \add promise\add*.
\s1 Paul’s concerns about their faith
\p
\v 8 When you didn’t know God, you served others who aren’t really gods at all.
\v 9 But now that you do know God, or rather now that God knows you, why would you turn back again to useless and inferior beliefs, to serve them all over again?
\v 10 You \add people\add* observe \add special\add* holidays and \add certain\add* months and seasons and years.
\v 11 I worry that maybe I’ve wasted my time helping you.
\p
\v 12 I implore you to become like me, brothers and sisters, because I’m also just like you. You haven’t wronged me in any way,
\v 13 but you know that it was a result of my fleshly weakness that I originally preached the good message to you.\f + \fr 4:13 \ft Scholars are unclear whether this might mean something like ill health, poor eyesight (see v15), or even a moral weakness, so we’ve left it as ambiguous in the translation as it is in the original.\f*
\v 14 Yet you didn’t mock or despise me, \add brothers and sisters,\add* for this trial of my flesh, but rather you received me as God’s messenger like you might have received \add the\add* messiah Jesus himself.
\v 15 So where are your generous attitudes \add now\add*? I know that back then, you would have almost dug your own eyes out to give them to me.
\v 16 Have I \add now\add* become your enemy just because I told you \add people\add* the truth?
\p
\v 17 Those \add others\add* have worked hard to \add try to win\add* you \add over\add*, but not for good \add reasons\add*—they want to cut you off so that you’ll be enthusiastic about them.
\v 18 It’s good to be enthusiastic about things that are always good, and not only at the times when I’m with you, \add brothers and sisters\add*.
\v 19 My dear ones, who I’m again labouring with in childbirth until \add the\add* messiah becomes part of you,
\v 20 I wanted to be there with you now, and to speak quite differently than this, because I’m puzzled about you.
\s1 The free line and the slaves
\p
\v 21 Are you \add people\add* who are under the law telling me that you’re not even hearing what the law is?
\v 22 It’s written \add in the scriptures\add* that Abraham had two sons: one son with the slave girl \add Hagar\add*, and one with the free woman.
\v 23 The slave girl’s son was born because of human actions, but in contrast, the free woman’s son was a result of God’s promise.
\v 24 So if we use this as an illustration, there are two agreements: one is from Mt. Sinai which relates to Hagar and gives birth to enslaved people.
\v 25 Hagar’s Mt. Sinai is in Arabia and corresponds to the present \add earthly\add* Jerusalem in slavery along with her children.
\v 26 However, the heavenly Jerusalem, which is our mother, is free,
\v 27 because it’s been written \add in the scriptures\add*:
\q1 ‘Be glad barren woman,
\q2 you who have never borne a child.
\q1 Be bold and shout,
\q2 you who have never been in labour,
\q1 because the lonely woman \add now\add* has many children,
\q2 even more than the woman with a husband.’
\p
\v 28 And you, brothers and sisters, are children of the promise, like Isaac,
\v 29 but back then the one born because of human actions persecuted the one born because of the spirit, and that’s how it continues now.
\v 30 But what do the scriptures say? ‘Send away the slave girl and her son, because the slave girl’s son won’t be getting the inheritance alongside the free woman’s son.’
\v 31 So, brothers and sisters, we’re not \add descendants\add* of the slave girl, but of the free woman.
\c 5
\s1 Don’t let the law destroy your faith
\p
\v 1 The messiah has freed us so we can have freedom, so stand firm and don’t get entangled again in the burden of slavery.
\p
\v 2 What’s more, I, Paul would add that if you \add men\add* got circumcised, then \add the\add* messiah wouldn’t be of any benefit.
\v 3 I’ll say it again \add clearly\add*: every man who \add gets himself\add* circumcised has put himself in debt to obeying all of the law.
\v 4 You were released from \add the\add* messiah when you moved to trusting in the law in order to be right with God—you fell away from grace at that point.
\v 5 We who are in \add the\add* spirit are eagerly waiting for the righteousness that we’re expecting to receive by faith,
\v 6 because in \add the\add* messiah Jesus, it’s not circumcision that achieves anything, nor uncircumcision, but faith worked out through love.
\p
\v 7 You \add people\add* were running the race so well—who held you back from believing the truth?
\v 8 It wasn’t the one who called you.
\v 9 A little bit of yeast can make a whole loaf of bread rise.
\v 10 I’m trusting in Yahweh that you won’t be thinking anything different \add from the truth\add*. The one who’ll bear the judgement is the one who’s been confusing you, whoever that was.
\p
\v 11 But, brothers and sisters, if I was still preaching the Jewish rules, why would I still be being persecuted? If I was still preaching that, then talking about dying on a post wouldn’t even be offensive.
\v 12 I wish that the ones upsetting you \add people by suggesting cutting off bits\add* would cut it all off.
\p
\v 13 Brothers and sisters, you were called into freedom. That doesn’t mean that you should use the freedom to do whatever your minds desire, but rather, you should be serving each other in love,
\v 14 because the entire law can be fulfilled by obeying one rule: Love your neighbour like yourself.
\v 15 But if you’re snapping at and bullying each other, watch out, or else you’ll destroy each other’s \add faith\add*.
\s1 Being led by God’s spirit
\p
\v 16 I’ll tell you: allow yourselves to be led by \add the\add* spirit, and then your earthly desires won’t triumph,
\v 17 because our earthly desires oppose the spirit, and \add the\add* spirit opposes our earthly desires—they oppose each other, so that’s why you end up doing things that you didn’t \add really\add* want to do.
\v 18 But if you’re being led by \add the\add* spirit then you’re not \add trying to earn your way to God by\add* obeying all of Moses’ rules.
\p
\v 19 The actions that come from our earthly desires include: sexual immorality, impurity, sensuality,
\v 20 idolatry, sorcery, hostility, strife, jealousies, rages, selfish ambitions, arguing, divisions,
\v 21 envy, drunkenness, carousing, and other similar things which I’ve mentioned previously. And as I \add also\add* said previously, the people that do these things won’t be inheriting God’s kingdom.
\p
\v 22 But the end result of having God’s spirit is \add to show\add* love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faith,
\v 23 gentleness, and self-control—none \add of\add* Moses’s laws are against any of these!
\v 24 And for \add the\add* messiah Jesus’ \add apprentices\add*, earthly desires need to be executed on a post, along with their passions and lusts.
\v 25 If we’re \add being led\add* by God’s spirit in our living, then we should also be aligning \add our values\add* by \add the\add* spirit.
\v 26 We have to take care not to become conceited, not to provoke each other, and not to envy each other.
\c 6
\s1 Helping each other
\p
\v 1 Brothers and sisters, if a person has been caught sinning, you spiritual people need to guide the person with a spirit of gentleness, at the same time looking at your own behaviour, in case you yourselves are also tempted.
\v 2 Help those who are going through difficulties or pain, and in doing so you’ll fulfil \add the\add* messiah’s law,
\v 3 because anyone who supposes themself to be someone special, but isn’t, is only deceiving themself.
\v 4 Evaluate your own actions, but keep your boasts to yourself and don’t tell others,
\v 5 because each person must face up to their own issues.
\p
\v 6 Those who are being taught should share all their blessings with those who are teaching.
\p
\v 7 Don’t be fooled—you can’t \add get away with\add* mocking God, because what you sow determines what you’ll harvest.
\v 8 Anyone who sows \add seed\add* to feed their earthly desires will harvest earthly corruption, but anyone who sows spiritual \add seeds\add* will harvest eternal life from the spirit.
\v 9 Don’t be discouraged from doing good \add things\add*, because we’ll have a \add good\add* harvest in due course if we don’t give up.
\v 10 So then, as time allows it, we should be working towards good for everybody, and especially for fellow believers.
\s1 Paul’s final advice
\p
\v 11 \sig Look how big these letters are when it’s my own handwriting.\sig*
\v 12 \sig There’s so many people that want to look good in earthly terms, and they’re the ones trying to persuade you that you have to fulfil all of Moses rules—they don’t want to be persecuted concerning \+add the\+add* messiah dying on a post.\sig*
\v 13 \sig Even those who are circumcised don’t obey all the rules, but they’d still like for your men to be circumcised so that they can claim them as followers.\sig*
\v 14 \sig As for me, may I never boast, except about our master Jesus \+add the\+add* messiah, \+add whose life was cut off on the\+add* post. Through him, it’s like the entire world has been cut off from me, and I’ve been cut off from the world,\sig*
\v 15 \sig because obeying Moses’s law is nothing, and not needing to obey it is nothing, but being a new creation \+add is everything\+add*.\sig*
\v 16 \sig And everyone who follows that principle, may peace and mercy be on them and on God’s Israel.\sig*
\p
\v 17 \sig As for the other troubles, don’t bother me with them, because I carry the marks on my body of \+add someone who follows\+add* Jesus.\sig*
\p
\v 18 \sig May the grace from our master Jesus \+add the\+add* messiah be with your spirit, brothers and sisters. That’s how I want it to be.\sig*
